DRV.searchIndex = [{"t": "Overview \u00b7 AMDVLK Driver Atlas", "p": "Overview", "u": "index.html", "x": "AMDVLK, from vkCmdDraw to the GPU ring An interactive guide to AMD's open-source Vulkan driver, built from its actual source code. Every class, function and file named here exists in the pinned checkouts, and every code panel links to the exact lines on GitHub or GitLab. Project status: discontinued, repositories archived AMDVLK is discontinued. The first line of the AMDVLK README reads \u201cThis project is discontinued\u201d (with a link to discussion #416). The GitHub API reports the xgl , pal , llpc and llvm-project (GPUOpen fork) repositories as archived (read-only). The top-level AMDVLK repository is not marked archived, but its README carries the notice. The final xgl/pal/llpc commits are dated 2025-04-29 . libdrm and the Linux amdgpu driver are actively developed. Their checkouts here are newer (2026) than the AMDVLK code, so kernel line numbers match the pinned Linux SHA, not necessarily the kernel AMDVLK was last tested on. See Sources, SHAs & status ."}, {"t": "The stack, layer by layer", "p": "Overview", "u": "index.html#stack", "x": "Click a layer for its responsibilities, its key classes and links into the code. On each topic page, a smaller copy of this diagram follows the stepper and highlights the layer the current step runs in. LLPC is drawn beside XGL because it isn't a layer that calls go through . It is a compiler library that XGL calls into ( Llpc::ICompiler::Create ) and that returns an ELF. PAL then loads that ELF."}, {"t": "Start here: a reading order", "p": "Overview", "u": "index.html#start-here", "x": "Architecture & loader entry : how the Vulkan loader finds the driver, how a VkDevice handle becomes a C++ object, and how XGL objects own PAL objects. Read this first; the patterns repeat everywhere. Memory allocation : the simplest end-to-end path through all six software layers, and the place to learn the BO/VA/residency vocabulary used in every later topic. Command buffers & PM4 : what recording a draw actually writes, with a bit-level breakdown of a real DRAW_INDEX_AUTO packet. Submission & sync : how those command chunks become IBs in a CS ioctl, how the kernel puts them on a ring, and how fences and semaphores map to DRM syncobjs. Shader compilation : the deepest topic: SPIR-V \u2192 LLVM IR \u2192 LGC \u2192 AMDGPU ISA \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL. Read it last, or first if compilers are your thing. Keep the glossary open. Dotted-underlined terms show definitions on hover. Press / to search."}, {"t": "The five traces", "p": "Overview", "u": "index.html#traces", "x": "TOPIC 1 Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL ICD entry, dispatch tables, handle wrapping, HWL selection, where LLPC is created. TOPIC 2 vkAllocateMemory Heaps \u2192 domains, VA reservation vs. BO creation vs. GEM_VA mapping, residency, mmap. TOPIC 3 Pipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF SPIRV reader, LGC passes, NGG, AMDGPU backend, PAL metadata, four cache layers. TOPIC 4 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4 Command chunks, function-pointer dispatch, packet builders, barriers. TOPIC 5 vkQueueSubmit \u2192 ring IBs, BO lists, CS chunks, scheduler, INDIRECT_BUFFER, syncobjs."}, {"t": "Five facts that surprise newcomers", "p": "Overview", "u": "index.html#findings", "x": "This PAL and LLPC cannot target GFX9 (Vega). PAL's GfxIpLevel starts at GfxIp10_1 and LLPC asserts gfxIp.major >= 10 . The AMDVLK README sends pre-GFX10 users to the 2023.Q3.3 release. This site therefore covers GFX10, GFX11 and GFX12. PAL's hw/gfxip/gfx9 directory is the GFX10\u2013GFX11.5 hardware layer. Device creation sends 10.1, 10.3, 11.0 and 11.5 to Gfx9::CreateDevice . Only GFX12 has its own layer. Reserving GPU virtual address space never reaches the kernel. libdrm's amdgpu_va_range_alloc is a user-space allocator. The kernel first sees the address when a BO is mapped there with DRM_AMDGPU_GEM_VA . Every VkDeviceMemory is resident for its whole life. XGL adds each allocation to PAL's device-global reference list at creation ( Device::AddMemReference ), and that list becomes the BO list of every submission. Shader modules aren't compiled to ISA. vkCreateShaderModule only hashes and preprocesses the SPIR-V. Codegen happens per pipeline , because LGC needs vertex formats, color formats and the descriptor layout to lower the shaders."}, {"t": "How to read the code panels", "p": "Overview", "u": "index.html#how-to-read", "x": "The header shows repo / path Lstart\u2013end . The link opens the full file at the pinned commit, with those lines highlighted. Excerpts are cut from the source by a script ( tools/build.py ), never retyped. They are at most 30 lines, so they often stop mid-function; follow the link for the rest. Each panel has an Explain note in plain language. Where something couldn't be traced from the cloned source, the page says not traced rather than guessing. Built from the pinned sources listed on Sources, SHAs & status . Excerpts \u00a9 their respective authors under their original licenses: MIT for AMDVLK, xgl, pal and llpc; MIT-style permission notices on the libdrm and Linux amdgpu files shown; Apache-2.0 with LLVM exceptions for LLVM. Individual files may carry their own headers."}, {"t": "Architecture &amp; loader entry \u00b7 AMDVLK Driver Atlas", "p": "Architecture &amp; loader entry", "u": "architecture.html", "x": "1 \u00b7 Architecture & loader entry Three AMD libraries make up the driver, and each has one job. XGL speaks Vulkan. PAL speaks GPU and OS. LLPC turns SPIR-V pipelines into machine code. This page shows how they connect and how a Vulkan call enters them."}, {"t": "Who does what", "p": "Architecture &amp; loader entry", "u": "architecture.html#roles", "x": "Component Repo / dir Owns Doesn't know about XGL xgl/icd/api Vulkan semantics: entry points, memory types, render passes, descriptor sets, barriers in Vulkan terms, pipeline caches. PM4, registers, ioctls. PAL pal/src/core Hardware and OS: GPU memory, command buffers as PM4, queues, fences. Per-generation HWLs in hw/gfxip/gfx9 and hw/gfxip/gfx12 . OS backend in os/amdgpu . Vulkan. PAL is shared with other AMD drivers. LLPC / LGC llpc/llpc , llpc/lgc SPIR-V \u2192 LLVM IR \u2192 AMDGPU ELF with PAL metadata. Uses AMD's LLVM fork for codegen. GPU memory or submission. It returns bytes; XGL hands them to PAL. The dependency arrows only point one way. XGL calls PAL interfaces ( Pal::IDevice , Pal::IGpuMemory , Pal::ICmdBuffer , Pal::IQueue ) and the LLPC interface ( Llpc::ICompiler ). PAL calls libdrm. The two libraries never call each other: the only thing that moves from LLPC to PAL is the ELF blob, and XGL carries it."}, {"t": "Step-through: from loader to PAL", "p": "Architecture &amp; loader entry", "u": "architecture.html#trace", "x": ""}, {"t": "Loader entry and dispatch tables", "p": "Architecture &amp; loader entry", "u": "architecture.html#loader", "x": "The Khronos loader reads amd-icd.json , loads the library, and calls vk_icdNegotiateLoaderICDInterfaceVersion . XGL accepts interface versions 3 through 6. From then on, every function pointer the loader hands the application comes from vk_icdGetInstanceProcAddr . XGL keeps three kinds of DispatchTable . The global table, g_GlobalDispatchTable , answers before an instance exists. Each instance has a table filtered by the enabled API version and instance extensions, and each device has one filtered by device extensions. Every table is filled by the same DispatchTable::Init , which pairs each name with the internal vk::entry:: implementation."}, {"t": "Handles are pointers; the loader owns the first word", "p": "Architecture &amp; loader entry", "u": "architecture.html#handles", "x": "For dispatchable handles ( VkInstance , VkPhysicalDevice , VkDevice , VkQueue , VkCommandBuffer ), the loader writes its own dispatch pointer into the first pointer-sized word of the object. XGL's Dispatchable<C> template reserves that word as VK_LOADER_DATA (seeded with ICD_LOADER_MAGIC ) and stores the real C++ object right after it. ObjectFromHandle converts a handle into a pointer to that object. Non-dispatchable handles ( VkDeviceMemory , VkBuffer , VkPipeline \u2026) need no loader word. NonDispatchable<> just casts the 64-bit handle to a pointer."}, {"t": "XGL objects wrap PAL objects", "p": "Architecture &amp; loader entry", "u": "architecture.html#wrapping", "x": "Almost every XGL object holds one PAL object per GPU in the device group, and PAL never allocates CPU memory for API objects itself. XGL first asks PAL how big its object is (e.g. GetGpuMemorySize , GetFenceSize , GetCmdAllocatorSize ), then usually makes one allocation (semaphores are an exception and allocate the two separately) through the application's VkAllocationCallbacks holding [XGL object][PAL object \u00d7 N GPUs] , and finally calls PAL's Create* with a placement address inside it. Vulkan handle XGL class PAL interface it owns VkInstance Instance Pal::IPlatform ( Pal::CreatePlatform ) VkPhysicalDevice / VkDevice PhysicalDevice / Device Pal::IDevice ( Device::PalDevice ) VkDeviceMemory Memory Pal::IGpuMemory VkCommandPool / VkCommandBuffer CmdPool / CmdBuffer Pal::ICmdAllocator / Pal::ICmdBuffer VkQueue Queue Pal::IQueue VkPipeline GraphicsPipeline / ComputePipeline Pal::IPipeline VkFence / VkSemaphore Fence / Semaphore Pal::IFence / Pal::IQueueSemaphore"}, {"t": "PAL picks a hardware layer and an OS layer", "p": "Architecture &amp; loader entry", "u": "architecture.html#hwl", "x": "Inside PAL, the generic Pal::Device creates a GfxDevice for the detected GFX IP ( device.cpp ). On Linux, the OS-specific object is an Amdgpu::Device that talks to libdrm through a table of dlsym 'd function pointers:"}, {"t": "Where LLPC is invoked", "p": "Architecture &amp; loader entry", "u": "architecture.html#llpc", "x": "Each PhysicalDevice has a PipelineCompiler . During initialization it converts PAL's GfxIpLevel into a Vkgc::GfxIpVersion and creates the Llpc::ICompiler ( compiler_solution_llpc.cpp ). After that, LLPC is called from exactly two places: BuildShaderModule (shader module creation) and BuildGraphicsPipeline / BuildComputePipeline (pipeline creation, on a cache miss). See Topic 3 ."}, {"t": "Which GPU generations?", "p": "Architecture &amp; loader entry", "u": "architecture.html#gfx", "x": "The pinned PAL enumerates only GFX10.1 and newer for current clients: GFX9 (Vega) support was removed from these final AMDVLK sources. The AMDVLK README says \u201cFor Pre-GFX10 GPUs, please use v-2023.Q3.3 or older release.\u201d Everything on this site therefore describes GFX10, GFX11 and GFX12."}, {"t": "Memory allocation \u00b7 AMDVLK Driver Atlas", "p": "Memory allocation", "u": "memory.html", "x": "2 \u00b7 Memory allocation vkAllocateMemory runs through every software layer: XGL picks a PAL heap, PAL reserves a GPU virtual address, creates a buffer object in the kernel and maps it at that address, and XGL marks the memory resident for every future submission."}, {"t": "Virtual address vs. physical backing: three separate operations", "p": "Memory allocation", "u": "memory.html#three-ops", "x": "On amdgpu, a usable allocation is the result of three independent steps, and PAL performs them separately in Amdgpu::GpuMemory::AllocateOrPinMemory : # What PAL call libdrm Kernel A Reserve a GPU VA range AssignVirtualAddress amdgpu_va_range_alloc none. The VA allocator lives in user space ( dev->va_mgr ). B Create physical backing (a BO in VRAM or GTT) AllocBuffer amdgpu_bo_alloc DRM_AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE \u2192 amdgpu_gem_create_ioctl C Map B at A in the process's GPU page tables MapVirtualAddress amdgpu_bo_va_op_raw DRM_AMDGPU_GEM_VA \u2192 amdgpu_gem_va_ioctl \u2192 amdgpu_vm_bo_map Keeping them separate is what makes virtual (sparse/PRT) allocations cheap. For memory created with flags.virtualAlloc , PAL does step A and then only reserves the range as PRT ( Device::ReservePrtVaRange ), with no backing. Sparse binding later maps real BOs into pieces of that range. A VkBuffer or VkImage bound to ordinary memory gets no VA of its own. Its address is the memory's VA plus the bind offset ( Buffer bind )."}, {"t": "Step-through: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 kernel", "p": "Memory allocation", "u": "memory.html#trace", "x": ""}, {"t": "How heaps and memory types are derived", "p": "Memory allocation", "u": "memory.html#heaps", "x": "PAL describes memory as four heaps . The kernel has two domains . Vulkan exposes memory heaps and memory types . The table connects them, using PAL's default heap flags ( Device::InitMemoryHeapProperties ), XGL's conversion ( PhysicalDevice memory-type setup ) and PAL's amdgpu translation ( heapToDomain / heapToFlags ): PAL heap PAL heap flags (default) Vulkan memory type flags Vulkan heap Kernel domain + create flags GpuHeapInvisible cpuUncached DEVICE_LOCAL VRAM (DEVICE_LOCAL) AMDGPU_GEM_DOMAIN_VRAM + AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE_NO_CPU_ACCESS GpuHeapLocal cpuVisible, cpuGpuCoherent, cpuUncached, cpuWriteCombined DEVICE_LOCAL | HOST_VISIBLE | HOST_COHERENT CPU-visible VRAM (DEVICE_LOCAL) AMDGPU_GEM_DOMAIN_VRAM + AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE_CPU_ACCESS_REQUIRED | AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE_CPU_GTT_USWC GpuHeapGartUswc cpuVisible, cpuGpuCoherent, cpuUncached, cpuWriteCombined HOST_VISIBLE | HOST_COHERENT system memory AMDGPU_GEM_DOMAIN_GTT + AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE_CPU_GTT_USWC GpuHeapGartCacheable cpuVisible, cpuGpuCoherent HOST_VISIBLE | HOST_COHERENT | HOST_CACHED shares the GartUswc heap AMDGPU_GEM_DOMAIN_GTT , no extra flag (CPU-cached) Order matters. XGL creates heaps and types in the fixed priority order Invisible, GartUswc, Local, GartCacheable, so memory type indices follow that order on a typical discrete GPU. Heap flags. PalGpuHeapToVkMemoryHeapFlags marks Local and Invisible as VK_MEMORY_HEAP_DEVICE_LOCAL_BIT . Resizable BAR. When PAL reports an Invisible heap of size 0 (all VRAM CPU-visible), XGL routes the Invisible memory type to GpuHeapLocal , and PAL's amdgpu layer treats Invisible like Local. \u201cLocal\u201d vs. \u201cInvisible\u201d is only a hint to the kernel. Both are VRAM. The CPU_ACCESS_REQUIRED / NO_CPU_ACCESS flags steer placement inside or outside the CPU-visible BAR window, and the PAL comment notes the kernel may change this at runtime. Device-coherent types for VK_AMD_device_coherent_memory are added as extra types when the GPU supports GL2-uncached access ( supportGl2Uncached )."}, {"t": "Residency, memory references and the BO list", "p": "Memory allocation", "u": "memory.html#residency", "x": "On amdgpu, a BO that the GPU will touch must either appear in the submission's BO list or be VM-always-valid . The kernel uses the list to validate (and, if needed, move back) every BO before running the job. Vulkan has no residency API, so XGL takes the simplest correct route: Device::AddMemReference is called for every allocation. It calls IDevice::AddGpuMemoryReferences with pQueue = nullptr , meaning \"device-global\". Amdgpu::Device::AddGpuMemoryReferences adds the memory to every queue's m_globalRefMap . Memory created VM_ALWAYS_VALID is skipped , because the kernel keeps it valid in the process VM. At submit time Queue::UpdateResourceList turns the map into the BO list passed with the CS ioctl (see Topic 5 ). PAL requests AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE_VM_ALWAYS_VALID when the kernel supports it and the memory is private to the process (not shareable, not exported, not flippable, not peer-writable). Those BOs share the VM root's reservation object ( kernel side ), so they never need to appear in the BO list."}, {"t": "Mapping host-visible memory", "p": "Memory allocation", "u": "memory.html#mapping", "x": "vkMapMemory \u2192 Memory::Map \u2192 IGpuMemory::Map . PAL refuses virtual memory and anything not CPU-visible ( ErrorNotMappable ), which is why Invisible-heap memory types lack HOST_VISIBLE. For a mappable BO, libdrm's amdgpu_bo_cpu_map asks the kernel ( DRM_AMDGPU_GEM_MMAP ) for the BO's fake mmap offset and then calls mmap on the DRM file descriptor. CPU accesses to VRAM pages go through the PCIe BAR; GTT pages are ordinary system pages, either write-combined (USWC) or cached. How the kernel services the resulting page faults (TTM) is not traced . Mappings are reference-counted inside libdrm per BO. XGL can keep a mapping alive for the object's lifetime with the skipUnMapMemory setting."}, {"t": "Not traced on this page", "p": "Memory allocation", "u": "memory.html#not-traced", "x": "TTM placement, eviction and the VRAM \u2192 GTT fallback inside amdgpu_bo_create . Page-table writing in amdgpu_gem_va_update_vm / amdgpu_vm_bo_update . Imported/exported memory ( OpenExternalMemory , dma-buf) and pinned host memory ( CreateGpuPinnedMemory \u2192 PinMemory ) are only mentioned, not stepped through."}, {"t": "Shader compilation \u00b7 AMDVLK Driver Atlas", "p": "Shader compilation", "u": "shaders.html", "x": "3 \u00b7 Shader compilation AMDVLK compiles pipelines , not shaders. A shader module is only hashed and preprocessed; when a pipeline is created, LLPC translates each stage's SPIR-V to LLVM IR, links the stages into one module, lowers it with full knowledge of the pipeline state, and has LLVM emit a single ELF that PAL loads."}, {"t": "The pipeline in one picture", "p": "Shader compilation", "u": "shaders.html#overview", "x": "XGL : vkCreateShaderModule \u2192 PipelineCompiler::BuildShaderModule \u2192 LLPC BuildShaderModule , which hashes the SPIR-V and records usage. No ISA is produced yet. XGL : vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 cache lookups \u2192 ConvertGraphicsPipelineInfo builds a Vkgc::GraphicsPipelineBuildInfo from all the Vulkan state. LLPC front end : per stage, LowerTranslator \u2192 SPIRV reader ( SPIRVToLLVM ) \u2192 front-end lowering passes. The stages are then linked into one pipeline module. LGC middle end : PipelineState::generate \u2192 LgcLowering::addPasses : state-dependent lowering, hardware stage mapping, NGG. LLVM AMDGPU backend : addPassesToEmitFile on target amdgcn--amdpal with CPU gfx10xx / gfx11xx / gfx12xx . It produces ISA plus a PAL metadata note, packaged as an ELF. PAL : IDevice::CreateGraphicsPipeline reads the ELF with PipelineAbiReader , uploads code with CodeObjectUploader , and turns the metadata into register state."}, {"t": "Step-through: shader module \u2192 pipeline \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "p": "Shader compilation", "u": "shaders.html#trace", "x": ""}, {"t": "SPIR-V \u2192 LLVM IR", "p": "Shader compilation", "u": "shaders.html#spirv", "x": "LowerTranslator is an LLVM module pass that runs the SPIRV reader on one shader stage. Before translating, it optionally runs a SPIR-V optimizer and builds the specialization-constant map from VkSpecializationInfo . llvm::readSpirv parses the binary into a SPIRVModule , and SPIRVToLLVM::translate picks the requested entry point and emits IR. The translator is constructed with an lgc::Builder , so resource and built-in operations become LGC operations that are resolved later, not raw hardware intrinsics. Front-end lowering ( Lowering::addPasses ) then cleans up SPIR-V constructs: CFG merges, aggressive inlining, access chains, terminators, globals, memory operations, math precision. Once every stage is lowered, the per-stage modules are linked into one pipeline module."}, {"t": "LGC: pipeline-state-dependent lowering", "p": "Shader compilation", "u": "shaders.html#lgc", "x": "LGC receives the pipeline state that XGL converted (vertex input formats, color target formats and blend, descriptor layout as user-data nodes, GFX IP, options) through setPipelineState . Its pass list ( LgcLowering::addPasses ) is where that state is consumed: Pass Needs which pipeline state LowerVertexFetch Vertex binding/attribute formats and strides, to generate fetch code for VS inputs. LowerFragmentColorExport Color attachment formats and blend, to pick export formats and conversions. LowerDesc Descriptor layout (resource mapping), to turn descriptor accesses into loads from user data/descriptor tables. LowerInOut Which stages exist and how they are merged, to lower inter-stage I/O. PreparePipelineAbi Which stages exist, to merge API stages into hardware stages and lay out the PAL ABI. CheckShaderCache Runs after CollectResourceUsage , so a stage can be skipped if an identical one was already compiled."}, {"t": "Where caching happens", "p": "Shader compilation", "u": "shaders.html#caching", "x": "Pipeline creation tries four lookups, roughly from cheapest to most expensive to miss: # Cache Key Code 1 XGL shader-module cache (in-memory map, then the internal binary cache) hash of SPIR-V LoadShaderModuleFromCache 2 XGL pipeline-binary cache: the app's VkPipelineCache first, then XGL's internal PipelineBinaryCache pipeline cache ID (MetroHash of create info) GetCachedPipelineBinary 3 LLPC whole-pipeline cache ( CacheFullPipelines , on by default) LLPC cacheHash BuildGraphicsPipeline 4 LGC per-stage cache ( EnablePerStageCache , on by default) per-stage hash CheckShaderCache pass LLPC's \"internal\" caches are XGL's PipelineBinaryCache exposed through a Vkgc::ICache adapter ( GetCacheAdapter ), unless the shaderCacheMode setting disables it. The AMDVLK README documents on-disk caching under ~/.cache/AMD/VkCache (setting AllowVkPipelineCachingToDisk ). PAL's archive-file layer behind that is not traced here."}, {"t": "AMDGPU backend \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "p": "Shader compilation", "u": "shaders.html#backend", "x": "LGC creates an LLVM TargetMachine for triple amdgcn--amdpal with a CPU name derived from the GFX IP ( getGpuNameString : {11,0,0} \u2192 gfx1100 ) and calls addPassesToEmitFile . Inside LLVM, AMDGPUAsmPrinter::EmitPALMetadata records each hardware stage's entry symbol and register usage into AMDGPUPALMetadata , which is written as a MsgPack note ( toBlob ). The instruction selection, register allocation and scheduling that produce the ISA are standard LLVM codegen and are not traced on this site. PAL never interprets the ISA. PipelineAbiReader finds the symbols and metadata; CodeObjectUploader copies the code into GPU memory and applies relocations; the HWL ( Gfx9 / Gfx12 GraphicsPipeline::HwlInit ) builds the register values it will later write with SET_SH_REG / SET_CONTEXT_REG when the pipeline is bound."}, {"t": "How the GFX IP level changes the output", "p": "Shader compilation", "u": "shaders.html#gfx", "x": "Aspect GFX10 (10.1, 10.3) GFX11 (11.0, 11.5) GFX12 Where LLVM CPU gfx1010 \u2026 gfx1036 gfx1100 \u2026 gfx1151 gfx1201 GpuNameMap NGG (primitive shaders) on unless NggFlagDisable mandatory mandatory LgcLowering Extra LGC passes \u2014 CollectImageOperations CollectImageOperations , AddBufferOperationMetadata \u2265 11 , \u2265 12 Buffer format encodings e.g. BUF_FORMAT_32_32_32_32_FLOAT_GFX10 e.g. BUF_FORMAT_32_32_32_32_FLOAT_GFX11 PreparePipelineAbi PAL HWL that loads it Gfx9::GraphicsPipeline (dir hw/gfxip/gfx9 ) Gfx12::GraphicsPipeline HWL selection GFX9 (Vega) Not supported by these sources: LLPC asserts gfxIp.major >= 10 ; no GfxIp9 for current PAL clients. ICompiler::Create Beyond these switches, most generation differences sit inside the LLVM backend (instruction encodings and scheduling models such as GFX12SpeedModel ) and are not traced here."}, {"t": "Command buffers &amp; PM4 \u00b7 AMDVLK Driver Atlas", "p": "Command buffers &amp; PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html", "x": "4 \u00b7 Command buffers & PM4 Recording a Vulkan command buffer means PAL writing PM4 packets into chunks of GPU-visible memory. Nothing reaches the kernel until submission. This page follows vkCmdDraw down to the three DWORDs it produces and takes them apart bit by bit."}, {"t": "Pools, allocators and chunks", "p": "Command buffers &amp; PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#chunks", "x": "Each VkCommandPool owns a PAL ICmdAllocator ( vk_cmd_pool.cpp ). The allocator manages separate pools for several kinds of data. All are GPU memory carved into uniform chunks : CommandDataAlloc holds the PM4 command streams themselves. EmbeddedDataAlloc / LargeEmbeddedDataAlloc hold \"embedded data\": tables and constants that PAL writes at record time for commands to reference (the large variant is for allocations over 32 KB). GpuScratchMemAlloc is GPU-only scratch for PAL's internal use. A CmdStream asks for space with ReserveCommands and gives back what it didn't use with CommitCommands . When a chunk fills, CmdStream::GetNextChunk takes one of the stream's retained chunks or calls CmdAllocator::GetNewChunk . The allocator pops its free list (known idle), then scans its reuse list for chunks whose GPU work has finished ( IsIdleOnGpu ), and only then creates a new CmdStreamAllocation , split into numChunks chunks. Successive chunks of one stream are chained with chaining packets at each chunk's tail, so at submit time only the stream's first chunk is handed to the kernel as an IB (see Topic 5 )."}, {"t": "The draw hot path", "p": "Command buffers &amp; PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#hot-path", "x": "XGL does little at draw time. CmdBuffer::Draw flushes any changed Vulkan dynamic state into PAL ( ValidateGraphicsStates ) and calls ICmdBuffer::CmdDraw . That call is an inline jump through m_funcTable.pfnCmdDraw , which the HWL fills with a template instantiation specialized for the GPU and the command buffer's options. The HWL's CmdDraw : Validates ( ValidateDraw ): writes dirty state as SET_SH_REG / SET_CONTEXT_REG packets. This covers the bound pipeline's registers, user-data SGPRs (descriptor table addresses, push constants), viewport/scissor, and draw-time values such as NUM_INSTANCES and the vertex offset . The full register sequence is not traced line by line. Builds the draw packet into reserved space: DRAW_INDEX_AUTO for non-indexed draws, IT_DRAW_INDEX_2 for indexed, IT_DRAW_INDIRECT / IT_DRAW_INDEX_INDIRECT or IT_EXECUTE_INDIRECT__EXECINDIRECT variants for indirect draws. Commits the space."}, {"t": "Step-through: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "p": "Command buffers &amp; PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#trace", "x": ""}, {"t": "Anatomy of a real packet: DRAW_INDEX_AUTO", "p": "Command buffers &amp; PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#pm4", "x": "This is the packet CmdUtil::BuildDrawIndexAuto writes for vkCmdDraw . The field layouts come from PAL's generated chip headers ( PM4_ME_TYPE_3_HEADER , PM4_PFP_DRAW_INDEX_AUTO , VGT_DRAW_INITIATOR ). Change the inputs to see the DWORDs change, and click a field to see what it means. Where the other fields come from: the instance count and first vertex/instance are not in this packet. PAL wrote them during validation as an IT_NUM_INSTANCES packet and as user-data SGPRs that the compiled vertex shader reads. That is why DRAW_INDEX_AUTO needs only a vertex count."}, {"t": "Compute: DISPATCH_DIRECT", "p": "Command buffers &amp; PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#dispatch", "x": "vkCmdDispatch follows the same shape: CmdBuffer::Dispatch \u2192 ICmdBuffer::CmdDispatch \u2192 HWL CmdDispatch \u2192 CmdUtil::BuildDispatchDirect . That builds a 5-DWORD packet (header, dim_x , dim_y , dim_z , dispatch_initiator ) with shaderType = ShaderCompute . On a compute-only queue the same builder writes into a ComputeCmdBuffer stream that runs on the MEC."}, {"t": "Barriers: ACQUIRE_MEM and RELEASE_MEM", "p": "Command buffers &amp; PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#barriers", "x": "On GFX10+ XGL expresses vkCmdPipelineBarrier in PAL's acquire/release model ( useReleaseAcquire ). PAL's BarrierMgr turns the source stages into release events (e.g. a bottom-of-pipe RELEASE_MEM event, optionally with cache write-back) and the destination stages into an acquire point (how early in the pipe the CP must wait). Cache invalidations go into an ACQUIRE_MEM . Image layout transitions may add decompression or clear blits through PAL's internal RPM ( hw/gfxip/rpm ), which is not traced here. One generation difference is visible in the code: on GFX11 the color/depth (RB) caches can no longer be flushed by an ACQUIRE_MEM ( assert )."}, {"t": "GFX12 differences", "p": "Command buffers &amp; PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#gfx12", "x": "GFX12 has its own HWL ( hw/gfxip/gfx12 ) with its own UniversalCmdBuffer , CmdUtil and packet headers. The draw packet is built the same way, but register writes are organized around pair packets:"}, {"t": "Submission &amp; sync \u00b7 AMDVLK Driver Atlas", "p": "Submission &amp; sync", "u": "submit.html", "x": "5 \u00b7 Submission & synchronization Submission turns recorded command chunks into one CS ioctl. The ioctl carries IB descriptors, a BO list and syncobj wait/signal lists. The kernel validates it, queues a job, and eventually writes an INDIRECT_BUFFER packet into a hardware ring. Fences and semaphores are DRM syncobjs throughout."}, {"t": "The path in one paragraph", "p": "Submission &amp; sync", "u": "submit.html#overview", "x": "vkQueueSubmit \u2192 Queue::Submit (XGL) queues the semaphore waits, collects each command buffer's Pal::ICmdBuffer , and calls IQueue::Submit . PAL's core Queue::SubmitInternal associates fences and calls OsSubmit . The amdgpu backend builds the BO list from residency references, turns each command stream into an IB, packs IB + syncobj + BO-list chunks, and calls libdrm amdgpu_cs_submit_raw2 , which issues DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS . In the kernel, amdgpu_cs_ioctl parses and validates and pushes a job to the DRM GPU scheduler. When its dependencies resolve, amdgpu_job_run \u2192 amdgpu_ib_schedule writes the IB pointers and a fence into the ring."}, {"t": "Step-through: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 ring, then fences & semaphores", "p": "Submission &amp; sync", "u": "submit.html#trace", "x": ""}, {"t": "IBs and rings", "p": "Submission &amp; sync", "u": "submit.html#ibs", "x": "An IB is just {va_start, ib_bytes, ip_type, ring, flags} ( drm_amdgpu_cs_chunk_ib ): a pointer to PM4 that PAL already wrote into GPU memory. PAL submits one IB per command stream: the stream's first chunk, with the remaining chunks reached by chaining. The IP type selects the ring family: Vulkan queue family PAL queue/engine ip_type Hardware GRAPHICS | COMPUTE | TRANSFER | SPARSE QueueTypeUniversal / EngineTypeUniversal AMDGPU_HW_IP_GFX GFX ring (CP PFP/ME) COMPUTE | TRANSFER | SPARSE QueueTypeCompute / EngineTypeCompute AMDGPU_HW_IP_COMPUTE compute rings (MEC / ACE) TRANSFER | SPARSE QueueTypeDma / EngineTypeDma AMDGPU_HW_IP_DMA SDMA ring (SDMA packets, not PM4) Each PAL queue owns a kernel context ( amdgpu_cs_ctx_create2 , via CreateCommandSubmissionContextRaw ). The CS ioctl names that context, and the kernel returns a per-context sequence number that identifies the submission."}, {"t": "BO lists", "p": "Submission &amp; sync", "u": "submit.html#bo-list", "x": "Every BO the GPU may access must be in the submission's BO list, unless it was created VM_ALWAYS_VALID . PAL builds the list in UpdateResourceList from the device-global references XGL registered at allocation time ( Topic 2 ) and reuses the previous list when nothing changed. On current kernels the list goes inline in the CS ioctl as an AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_BO_HANDLES chunk. On the legacy path PAL first creates a kernel list object ( DRM_AMDGPU_BO_LIST , via amdgpu_bo_list_create_raw ) and passes its handle instead. In the kernel, amdgpu_cs_parser_bos locks and validates every listed BO before the job may run."}, {"t": "Fences, semaphores, timelines \u2192 syncobjs", "p": "Submission &amp; sync", "u": "submit.html#sync", "x": "Vulkan XGL \u2192 PAL amdgpu backend GPU-side wait / signal Host-side wait / signal VkFence Fence \u2192 Pal::IFence SyncobjFence (one syncobj); legacy TimestampFence on old kernels signal: after the CS, the queue's m_lastSignaledSyncObject fence is transferred into the fence's syncobj vkWaitForFences \u2192 WaitForSyncobjFences \u2192 amdgpu_cs_syncobj_wait binary VkSemaphore Semaphore \u2192 Pal::IQueueSemaphore QueueSemaphore (one syncobj) wait: AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_SYNCOBJ_TIMELINE_WAIT chunk at point 0; signal: transfer from the last-submit syncobj \u2014 (binary semaphores have no host operations) timeline VkSemaphore same, with flags.timeline same syncobj used as a timeline wait: AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_SYNCOBJ_TIMELINE_WAIT at the requested point; signal: transfer into the requested point vkWaitSemaphores / vkSignalSemaphore \u2192 WaitSemaphoreValue / SignalSemaphoreValue \u2192 amdgpu_cs_syncobj_timeline_wait / amdgpu_cs_syncobj_timeline_signal The design rests on one trick: every CS ioctl signals the queue's private syncobj ( m_lastSignaledSyncObject , appended to the signal chunk). Anything that must signal when that submission finishes, whether a VkFence or a Vulkan signal semaphore, gets a copy of that fence through ConveySyncObjectState . That function uses amdgpu_cs_syncobj_transfer , or a sync-file export/import as a fallback. Waits are never done on the CPU: they become kernel scheduler dependencies, so a submission blocked on a semaphore costs no CPU time. Ioctl-level detail for the syncobj chunks is in the kernel's amdgpu_cs_p2_syncobj_in , amdgpu_cs_p2_syncobj_timeline_wait , amdgpu_cs_p2_syncobj_out and amdgpu_cs_p2_syncobj_timeline_signal ( amdgpu_cs.c )."}, {"t": "The kernel side", "p": "Submission &amp; sync", "u": "submit.html#kernel", "x": "amdgpu_cs_ioctl runs a fixed sequence: amdgpu_cs_parser_init \u2192 amdgpu_cs_pass1 (copy and parse chunks) \u2192 amdgpu_cs_pass2 (IBs, syncobj dependencies) \u2192 amdgpu_cs_parser_bos (lock and validate BOs) \u2192 amdgpu_cs_patch_jobs \u2192 amdgpu_cs_vm_handling (page tables up to date) \u2192 amdgpu_cs_sync_rings \u2192 amdgpu_cs_submit , which calls drm_sched_entity_push_job . The DRM scheduler later calls amdgpu_job_run , which calls amdgpu_ib_schedule . For each IB, the ring's emit_ib (e.g. gfx_v11_0_ring_emit_ib_gfx ) writes an INDIRECT_BUFFER packet with the IB address and the process's VMID. The CP then executes PAL's PM4 using the process's page tables. The kernel checkout is newer (2026) than the AMDVLK sources (2025-04). Newer uAPI (e.g. AMDGPU_USERQ user-mode queues, visible in the ioctl table) is never issued by PAL's amdgpu backend in these sources."}, {"t": "Glossary \u00b7 AMDVLK Driver Atlas", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html", "x": "Glossary Elsewhere on the site, these terms are dotted-underlined. Hover over one, or focus it with the keyboard, to see the definition."}, {"t": "Sources, SHAs &amp; status \u00b7 AMDVLK Driver Atlas", "p": "Sources, SHAs &amp; status", "u": "versions.html", "x": "Sources, SHAs & status Every code panel and source link on this site points at the commits below. Excerpts are cut from these checkouts by tools/build.py , and tools/verify.py checks every identifier and reference against them."}, {"t": "Pinned commits", "p": "Sources, SHAs &amp; status", "u": "versions.html#shas", "x": "The same table, with branch names and dates, is in src/VERSIONS.md . xgl, pal and llpc were cloned from branch dev , which AMDVLK's default.xml manifest pins; at clone time dev and master pointed to the same final commit. The LLVM fork is the manifest's amd-gfx-gpuopen-dev branch, sparse-checked-out to llvm/lib/Target/AMDGPU . Linux is a sparse checkout of drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu and include/uapi/drm ."}, {"t": "Archive / discontinuation status (checked 2026-10-14)", "p": "Sources, SHAs &amp; status", "u": "versions.html#status", "x": "Repository GitHub \u201carchived\u201d README / notes Last commit AMDVLK no README line 2: \u201cThis project is discontinued\u201d (links discussion #416) 2025-09-15 xgl archived no notice in README 2025-04-29 pal archived no notice in README 2025-04-29 llpc archived no notice in README 2025-04-29 llvm-project (GPUOpen) archived AMD's LLVM fork used by LLPC 2025-04-22 libdrm (freedesktop GitLab) active \u2014 2026-09-17 linux (torvalds) active \u2014 2026-10-14 Because libdrm and Linux are much newer than the frozen AMDVLK code, the kernel and libdrm line numbers match today's code, which may differ from the versions AMDVLK last shipped against. The interfaces used here (GEM create/VA/mmap, CS with chunks, syncobjs) are long-standing uAPI."}, {"t": "Not traced, or traced only to the entry point", "p": "Sources, SHAs &amp; status", "u": "versions.html#not-traced", "x": "Vulkan loader internals : the Khronos loader isn't in any cloned repo. Only the ICD interface XGL exports is shown. PAL ValidateDraw register sequence : shown at the call site only. LLVM AMDGPU instruction selection, register allocation and scheduling : entry points only ( addPassesToEmitFile , AMDGPUAsmPrinter , PAL metadata). gpurt (ray-tracing library), MetroHash and CWPack : listed in the manifest but not cloned. Ray-tracing pipelines are out of scope. PAL's on-disk archive-file pipeline cache : mentioned only. Kernel memory management (TTM placement and eviction, page-table updates, mmap fault path) and the GPU scheduler's internals: entry functions only. GFX9 (Vega) : support was removed from these sources, so there is nothing to trace. Barrier-driven decompression blits (RPM) and image layout handling : not stepped through."}, {"t": "How this site was built and checked", "p": "Sources, SHAs &amp; status", "u": "versions.html#method", "x": "Read the code and wrote the call chains ( PLAN.md ). tools/spec.py describes each step by anchor text , not line number. tools/build.py finds the anchor in the source, cuts the excerpt (\u2264 30 lines), checks that the listed symbols appear inside it, and builds a SHA-pinned permalink. tools/verify.py extracts every <code> identifier and every reference from the pages and the generated data, and checks each against the checkouts. It also checks internal links and anchors. A headless Chromium run clicks through the diagram, every stepper, search, the PM4 widget and dark mode."}, {"t": "The loader finds the driver through a JSON manifest", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-1", "x": "The Khronos loader scans ICD manifest directories and reads  library_path . The  @AMDVLK_INSTALL_PATH@ / @ISABITS@  placeholders are filled in at build time (typically  amdvlk64.so ). The same file also declares an implicit AMD switchable-graphics layer. icd/Loader/LunarG/Lnx/amd-icd.json library_path"}, {"t": "Interface-version handshake", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-2", "x": "The first call the loader makes into the ICD. XGL accepts loader/ICD interface versions 3 to 6 and returns the highest one both sides support. Version 3+ means handles are dispatchable objects whose first word the loader owns. icd/api/vk_dispatch.cpp MinDriverSupportedInterfaceVersion MaxDriverSupportedInterfaceVersion"}, {"t": "vk_icdGetInstanceProcAddr", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-3", "x": "The ICD-exported symbol the loader uses to fetch every other entry point. It just forwards to XGL's internal  vk::entry::vkGetInstanceProcAddr . icd/api/vk_dispatch.cpp vk_icdGetInstanceProcAddr vk::entry::vkGetInstanceProcAddr"}, {"t": "Global vs. instance dispatch table", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-4", "x": "With no instance (e.g. looking up  vkCreateInstance ), XGL answers from the static  g_GlobalDispatchTable . Once an instance exists, it uses that instance's own table, which takes the enabled API version and extensions into account. icd/api/vk_dispatch.cpp g_GlobalDispatchTable GetDispatchTable"}, {"t": "Name \u2192 function pointer lookup", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-5", "x": "A linear scan of  g_EntryPointMetadataTable  by name. Each entry is typed GLOBAL, INSTANCE, PHYSDEVICE or DEVICE, and the table type decides which kinds may be returned. That is how  vkGetDeviceProcAddr  refuses instance-level functions unless the  lenientInstanceFuncQuery  setting is on. icd/api/vk_dispatch.cpp g_EntryPointMetadataTable EntryPoint::Type::GLOBAL EntryPoint::Type::PHYSDEVICE"}, {"t": "Filling the dispatch table", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-6", "x": " INIT_DISPATCH_ENTRY(vkAllocateMemory)  stores XGL's  vk::entry::vkAllocateMemory  in the table slot for that name. These  entry::  functions are XGL's real implementations, found in files such as  vk_device.cpp ,  vk_queue.cpp  and  entry.cpp . icd/api/vk_dispatch.cpp INIT_DISPATCH_ENTRY(vkAllocateMemory"}, {"t": "Dispatchable<C>: the loader owns the first word", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-7", "x": "A  VkDevice ,  VkQueue  or  VkCommandBuffer  handle points at a  Dispatchable<C> . Its first member,  VK_LOADER_DATA , starts as  ICD_LOADER_MAGIC  and is later overwritten by the loader with its dispatch pointer. The real driver object C sits right after it in raw storage, so C can use virtual functions and inheritance freely. icd/api/include/vk_dispatch.h VK_LOADER_DATA ICD_LOADER_MAGIC"}, {"t": "A typical entry point: handle \u2192 object \u2192 method", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-8", "x": "Almost every XGL entry point has this shape: convert the handle with  ApiDevice::ObjectFromHandle  (pointer arithmetic past the loader word), choose allocation callbacks, and call a method on the C++ object. icd/api/vk_device.cpp ApiDevice::ObjectFromHandle AllocMemory"}, {"t": "Instance creates the PAL platform", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-9", "x": " vkCreateInstance  ends up creating one  Pal::IPlatform . PAL uses the same \"ask the size, then construct in caller memory\" pattern everywhere:  Pal::GetPlatformSize()  first, then  Pal::CreatePlatform  into that memory. icd/api/vk_instance.cpp Pal::CreatePlatform m_pPalPlatform"}, {"t": "One Pal::IDevice per GPU", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-10", "x": " vkEnumeratePhysicalDevices  asks the PAL platform for its devices. On Linux each  Pal::IDevice  is an  Amdgpu::Device  that has opened a DRM render node and called  amdgpu_device_initialize . icd/api/vk_physical_device_manager.cpp EnumerateDevices"}, {"t": "XGL objects hold PAL objects", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-11", "x": "A logical  Device  keeps per-GPU state in  m_perGpu[] ; a device group has several GPUs. The same idea appears all over XGL:  Memory::PalMemory(idx) ,  CmdBuffer::PalCmdBuffer(idx) ,  Fence::PalFence(idx) . icd/api/include/vk_device.h m_perGpu[idx].pPalDevice"}, {"t": "Vulkan object and PAL object in one allocation", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-12", "x": "XGL asks PAL how big its object is ( GetGpuMemorySize ), makes one allocation through the app's  VkAllocationCallbacks  that holds both  sizeof(Memory)  and N PAL objects, and lets PAL construct its object in place. PAL never calls malloc for API objects. icd/api/vk_memory.cpp GetGpuMemorySize AllocApiObject"}, {"t": "PAL picks a hardware layer", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-13", "x": "Inside PAL, a  GfxDevice  is created for the detected GFX IP. Note the naming trap:  Gfx9::CreateDevice  handles  GFX10.1 through GFX11.5 . The directory is called gfx9 for historical reasons. Only GFX12 has its own HWL. src/core/device.cpp Gfx9::CreateDevice Gfx12::CreateDevice GfxIp11_5"}, {"t": "XGL derives the compiler's GFX IP from PAL", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-14", "x": " PipelineCompiler::Initialize  converts PAL's  GfxIpLevel  into a  Vkgc::GfxIpVersion  {major, minor, stepping}. LGC later turns that into an LLVM CPU name such as  gfx1100 . icd/api/pipeline_compiler.cpp m_gfxIp.major = 11 m_gfxIp.major = 12"}, {"t": "Where LLPC is created", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-15", "x": "Each physical device owns one  Llpc::ICompiler , created with the GFX IP and a list of LLVM-style options. From here on, every shader module and pipeline build goes through  m_pLlpc . icd/api/compiler_solution_llpc.cpp Llpc::ICompiler::Create m_pLlpc"}, {"t": "vkAllocateMemory", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-1", "x": "The entry point unwraps the  VkDevice  and forwards to  Device::AllocMemory , which forwards to the static  Memory::Create . icd/api/vk_device.cpp AllocMemory"}, {"t": "Memory type index \u2192 PAL heap list", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-2", "x": "The app's  memoryTypeIndex  selects the preferred PAL heap through a table built at device init. XGL may then append fallback heaps, for example  GpuHeapGartUswc  after Local/Invisible when overallocation is allowed. (On Linux, PAL's amdgpu backend uses only  heaps[0]  to pick the kernel domain; see the domain-table step.) icd/api/vk_memory.cpp GetPalHeapFromVkTypeIndex Pal::GpuHeapGartUswc"}, {"t": "How Vulkan heaps are derived from PAL heaps", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-3", "x": "At device init XGL walks PAL's four heaps in a fixed priority order and creates one  VkMemoryHeap  per heap with nonzero size.  GartCacheable is never a heap of its own : it shares GartUswc's heap, because both are the same system memory with different CPU caching. icd/api/vk_physical_device.cpp Pal::GpuHeapInvisible Pal::GpuHeapGartUswc Pal::GpuHeapLocal Pal::GpuHeapGartCacheable"}, {"t": "How Vulkan memory-type flags are derived", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-4", "x": "Each PAL heap becomes one memory type, and its flags come straight from PAL's heap properties.  cpuVisible  \u2192 HOST_VISIBLE,  cpuGpuCoherent  \u2192 HOST_COHERENT, not  cpuUncached  \u2192 HOST_CACHED (GartCacheable). A device-local heap \u2192 DEVICE_LOCAL. With PAL's default heap flags ( Device::InitMemoryHeapProperties ) a discrete GPU gets: Invisible = DEVICE_LOCAL; Local = DEVICE_LOCAL|HOST_VISIBLE|HOST_COHERENT (the BAR window, or all of VRAM with Resizable BAR); GartUswc = HOST_VISIBLE|HOST_COHERENT; GartCacheable = HOST_VISIBLE|HOST_COHERENT|HOST_CACHED. icd/api/vk_physical_device.cpp VK_MEMORY_PROPERTY_HOST_VISIBLE_BIT VK_MEMORY_PROPERTY_HOST_CACHED_BIT VK_MEMORY_PROPERTY_DEVICE_LOCAL_BIT"}, {"t": "Create the PAL object and add it to the residency list", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-5", "x": "For each GPU in the allocation mask XGL calls  Pal::IDevice::CreateGpuMemory  into placement memory. It then immediately calls  Device::AddMemReference . Vulkan has no explicit residency API, so XGL makes  every   VkDeviceMemory  resident for the device's whole lifetime. icd/api/vk_memory.cpp CreateGpuMemory AddMemReference"}, {"t": "Residency: AddGpuMemoryReferences", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-6", "x": "A  Pal::GpuMemoryRef  goes to  IDevice::AddGpuMemoryReferences  with  pQueue == nullptr , meaning a  device-global  reference. On amdgpu this lands in every queue's global reference map, which becomes the BO list of every later submission. Topic 5 shows the other end. icd/api/vk_device.cpp AddGpuMemoryReferences Pal::GpuMemoryRef"}, {"t": "PAL core: construct + Init", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-7", "x": " ConstructGpuMemoryObject  is virtual: on Linux it placement-news an  Amdgpu::GpuMemory . The generic  GpuMemory::Init  computes size, alignment and VA partition, then calls the OS-specific  AllocateOrPinMemory . src/core/device.cpp ConstructGpuMemoryObject Init(createInfo, internalInfo)"}, {"t": "Virtual vs. real allocations", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-8", "x": "PAL distinguishes  virtual  allocations ( flags.virtualAlloc ), which are only a VA range with no physical backing, used for sparse resources, from ordinary ones. Every allocation gets a VA. Only non-virtual ones also get a BO. src/core/gpuMemory.cpp m_desc.flags.isVirtual virtualAlloc"}, {"t": "Generic Init hands off to the OS layer", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-9", "x": "By now  baseVirtAddr  is either 0 (\"pick one for me\"), a start-VA hint, or a capture/replay address. Everything OS-specific happens in  AllocateOrPinMemory . src/core/gpuMemory.cpp AllocateOrPinMemory"}, {"t": "Step A: reserve a GPU virtual address", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-10", "x": "Unless the VA was pre-reserved (multi-GPU global VA) or this is an SVM allocation, PAL first reserves VA space with  Device::AssignVirtualAddress . So far no physical memory exists. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuGpuMemory.cpp AssignVirtualAddress IsVirtual() == false"}, {"t": "AssignVirtualAddress \u2192 amdgpu_va_range_alloc", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-11", "x": "For the default partition PAL calls libdrm's  amdgpu_va_range_alloc  (through  m_drmProcs ) and keeps the returned  amdgpu_va_handle . Descriptor-table and capture/replay partitions use PAL's own VAM manager instead. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuDevice.cpp pfnAmdgpuVaRangeAlloc amdgpu_gpu_va_range_general"}, {"t": "libdrm: VA allocation is pure user-space bookkeeping", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-12", "x": " Key point:   amdgpu_va_range_alloc  just carves a range out of libdrm's in-process allocator ( dev->va_mgr ). There is  no ioctl . The kernel learns about this VA only when a BO is mapped there with  AMDGPU_GEM_VA . amdgpu/amdgpu_vamgr.c amdgpu_va_range_alloc2 va_mgr"}, {"t": "Step B: PAL heap \u2192 kernel domain + flags", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-13", "x": "Where the four PAL heaps meet the kernel's two domains. Local and Invisible are both  AMDGPU_GEM_DOMAIN_VRAM ; the difference is a  hint ,  CPU_ACCESS_REQUIRED  vs.  NO_CPU_ACCESS . Both GART heaps are  AMDGPU_GEM_DOMAIN_GTT ; the difference is  CPU_GTT_USWC  (write-combined) vs. 0 (cached). With Resizable BAR (invisible heap size 0), Invisible is treated like Local. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuGpuMemory.cpp AMDGPU_GEM_DOMAIN_VRAM AMDGPU_GEM_DOMAIN_GTT AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE_NO_CPU_ACCESS AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE_CPU_GTT_USWC"}, {"t": "Allocate physical backing, then map it at the VA", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-14", "x": "Physical backing:  AllocBuffer  creates the BO. Then  MapVirtualAddress  binds it at the VA from step A. These three operations (VA reservation, BO creation, mapping) are independent on amdgpu, which is what makes sparse binding possible. Earlier, PAL may add  AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE_VM_ALWAYS_VALID  for BOs private to this process, so they never need to appear in a BO list. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuGpuMemory.cpp AllocBuffer MapVirtualAddress"}, {"t": "Device::AllocBuffer", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-15", "x": "A one-liner around libdrm's  amdgpu_bo_alloc . Failure is reported as  ErrorOutOfGpuMemory , which XGL turns into  VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DEVICE_MEMORY . src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuDevice.cpp pfnAmdgpuBoAlloc"}, {"t": "libdrm: amdgpu_bo_alloc \u2192 DRM_AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-16", "x": "Fills  drm_amdgpu_gem_create  (size, alignment, domains, flags) and issues the ioctl. The kernel returns a GEM handle, which libdrm wraps in an  amdgpu_bo_handle . amdgpu/amdgpu_bo.c DRM_AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE drmCommandWriteRead"}, {"t": "Kernel: amdgpu_gem_create_ioctl", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-17", "x": "The kernel validates flags and domains and always clears VRAM. A  VM_ALWAYS_VALID  BO shares the reservation object of the process's VM root BO, which is why it needs no BO list. Then  amdgpu_gem_object_create  \u2192  amdgpu_bo_create_user  creates the TTM BO (placement details are not traced here). drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_gem.c amdgpu_gem_object_create vm->root.bo"}, {"t": "Device::MapVirtualAddress", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-18", "x": "Maps the BO at the chosen VA with read/write/execute permission plus an MTYPE (cache policy) derived from the PAL memory's  MType . The MALL no-alloc flag is added when the policy says  Never . src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuDevice.cpp AMDGPU_VA_OP_MAP pfnAmdgpuBoVaOpRaw"}, {"t": "libdrm: amdgpu_bo_va_op_raw \u2192 DRM_AMDGPU_GEM_VA", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-19", "x": "This is the first time the kernel hears about the VA:  va_address ,  offset_in_bo  and  map_size  are sent with  AMDGPU_VA_OP_MAP . amdgpu/amdgpu_bo.c DRM_AMDGPU_GEM_VA va.va_address"}, {"t": "Kernel: amdgpu_gem_va_ioctl \u2192 amdgpu_vm_bo_map", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-20", "x": "The kernel records the mapping in the process VM ( amdgpu_vm_bo_map ). Shortly after this switch,  amdgpu_gem_va_update_vm  writes the page-table entries, unless user space asked for  AMDGPU_VM_DELAY_UPDATE . drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_gem.c amdgpu_vm_bo_map"}, {"t": "vkMapMemory \u2192 Memory::Map", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-21", "x": "Host-visible memory is mapped on demand. Multi-instance allocations cannot be mapped. With the  skipUnMapMemory  setting, XGL keeps the CPU pointer for the object's lifetime. icd/api/vk_memory.cpp PalMemory(m_primaryDeviceIndex)->Map"}, {"t": "PAL GpuMemory::Map: only CPU-visible heaps map", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-22", "x": "Virtual memory returns  ErrorUnavailable , and non-CPU-visible memory (the Invisible heap) returns  ErrorNotMappable . That is why only HOST_VISIBLE memory types can be mapped in Vulkan. Pinned host memory just returns the app's own pointer. src/core/gpuMemory.cpp IsCpuVisible OsMap ErrorNotMappable"}, {"t": "Amdgpu::Device::Map \u2192 amdgpu_bo_cpu_map", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-23", "x": " Amdgpu::GpuMemory::OsMap  calls this with the BO handle. For memory shared across devices in one process, it uses the  exporting  device's fd (the comment in  OsMap  explains the reverse-mapping problem). src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuDevice.cpp pfnAmdgpuBoCpuMap"}, {"t": "libdrm: GEM_MMAP for an offset, then mmap(2)", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-24", "x": "Two steps.  DRM_AMDGPU_GEM_MMAP  returns a fake file offset that identifies the BO, then  drm_mmap  maps the DRM fd at that offset. Mappings are reference-counted per BO. CPU page faults are served by the kernel from VRAM (through the BAR) or from system pages. amdgpu/amdgpu_bo.c DRM_AMDGPU_GEM_MMAP drm_mmap"}, {"t": "Kernel: amdgpu_gem_mmap_ioctl", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-25", "x": "Just returns the BO's mmap offset ( amdgpu_mode_dumb_mmap ). The actual mapping is created when user space calls mmap on the DRM fd. The TTM fault path is  not traced  here. drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_gem.c amdgpu_mode_dumb_mmap"}, {"t": "vkCreateShaderModule", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-1", "x": "Forwards through  Device::CreateShaderModule  to  ShaderModule::Create , which copies the SPIR-V and calls  ShaderModule::Init . icd/api/vk_device.cpp CreateShaderModule"}, {"t": "ShaderModule::Init", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-2", "x": "The SPIR-V words are wrapped in a  Vkgc::BinaryData  and handed to the per-device  PipelineCompiler . No ISA is generated here. In Vulkan the real work happens at pipeline creation, when all the state is known. icd/api/vk_shader.cpp BuildShaderModule Vkgc::BinaryData"}, {"t": "Shader-module cache", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-3", "x": " Cache #1 (shader modules) , keyed by a hash of the SPIR-V: an in-memory  m_shaderModuleHandleMap , then the internal  m_pBinaryCache . On a miss it calls LLPC and stores the result. icd/api/pipeline_compiler.cpp LoadShaderModuleFromCache StoreShaderModuleToCache m_compilerSolutionLlpc.BuildShaderModule"}, {"t": "Into LLPC: BuildShaderModule", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-4", "x": "XGL supplies an output allocator callback ( AllocateShaderOutput ) so LLPC's output lives in XGL-owned memory. icd/api/compiler_solution_llpc.cpp m_pLlpc->BuildShaderModule pfnOutputAlloc"}, {"t": "LLPC: hash, check cache, preprocess SPIR-V", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-5", "x": "LLPC hashes the SPIR-V with MetroHash and checks its own internal cache. On a miss (further down in the function) it identifies the binary type and collects usage info into a  ShaderModuleData . It still does not translate to LLVM IR. llpc/context/llpcCompiler.cpp CacheAccessor MetroHash64::Hash"}, {"t": "vkCreateGraphicsPipelines", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-6", "x": " Device::CreateGraphicsPipelines  loops over the create infos and calls  GraphicsPipelineCommon::Create  for each. icd/api/vk_device.cpp CreateGraphicsPipelines"}, {"t": "Library or full pipeline", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-7", "x": "Graphics pipeline libraries ( VK_EXT_graphics_pipeline_library ) take a separate path. This trace follows the monolithic  GraphicsPipeline::Create . icd/api/graphics_pipeline_common.cpp GraphicsPipeline::Create GraphicsPipelineLibrary::Create"}, {"t": "Pipeline binary cache lookup", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-8", "x": " Cache #2 (pipeline ELF) :  GetCachedPipelineBinary  checks the app's  VkPipelineCache  first, then XGL's internal  PipelineBinaryCache . If the ELF is found, LLPC is never called. icd/api/vk_graphics_pipeline.cpp GetCachedPipelineBinary shouldCompile"}, {"t": "Vulkan state \u2192 Vkgc::GraphicsPipelineBuildInfo", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-9", "x": "On a miss, XGL converts all relevant Vulkan state into LLPC's input: vertex input formats, color attachment formats, blend, resource mapping (descriptor layout \u2192 user-data nodes), shader stages and specialization constants. LGC's pipeline-state-dependent passes consume this state. icd/api/pipeline_compiler.cpp ConvertGraphicsPipelineInfo"}, {"t": "PipelineCompiler \u2192 compiler solution", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-10", "x": "XGL has a \"compiler solution\" abstraction. In this open-source tree the only one is  CompilerSolutionLlpc . icd/api/pipeline_compiler.cpp GetSolution(pCreateInfo->compilerType)->CreateGraphicsPipelineBinary"}, {"t": "Hand-off to LLPC: BuildGraphicsPipeline", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-11", "x": "Unless pre-compiled library ELFs are being linked, XGL asks LLPC to compile the whole pipeline in one go. icd/api/compiler_solution_llpc.cpp m_pLlpc->BuildGraphicsPipeline"}, {"t": "LLPC's own pipeline cache", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-12", "x": " Cache #3 (LLPC internal) : a whole-pipeline cache keyed by  cacheHash , which is the pipeline hash including state that affects code generation. On a miss LLPC builds a  GraphicsContext  and compiles. llpc/context/llpcCompiler.cpp cacheAccessor buildGraphicsPipelineInternal"}, {"t": "Create the LGC pipeline and give it the state", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-13", "x": " buildPipelineInternal  creates an  lgc::Pipeline  and copies the Vulkan pipeline state into it ( setPipelineState ). It also creates the  lgc::Builder  that the SPIR-V reader uses to emit LGC operations instead of raw hardware-specific IR. llpc/context/llpcCompiler.cpp createPipeline setPipelineState createBuilder"}, {"t": "Per stage: SPIR-V translation pass", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-14", "x": "For each shader stage, LLPC runs a small pass manager whose main pass is  LowerTranslator . It turns that stage's SPIR-V into an LLVM module. llpc/context/llpcCompiler.cpp LowerTranslator"}, {"t": "LowerTranslator \u2192 readSpirv", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-15", "x": "Before this, the function optionally runs a SPIR-V optimizer, builds the specialization-constant map and collects YCbCr converting samplers. Then the SPIRV reader does the translation. The excerpt is from  LowerTranslator::translateSpirvToLlvm . llpc/lowering/LowerTranslator.cpp readSpirv"}, {"t": "SPIRV reader: parse, then SPIRVToLLVM", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-16", "x": "Parses the binary into a  SPIRVModule  ( is >> *bm ), then  SPIRVToLLVM::translate  walks it and emits LLVM IR. The translator is given the  lgc::Builder , so image, built-in and similar operations come out as LGC builder calls rather than hardware-specific IR. LGC lowers them later, once it knows the pipeline state. llpc/translator/lib/SPIRV/SPIRVReader.cpp SPIRVToLLVM btl btl.translate"}, {"t": "SPIRVToLLVM::translate", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-17", "x": "Picks the requested entry point for this stage and starts translating. Only that entry point's call graph ends up in the stage's module. llpc/translator/lib/SPIRV/SPIRVReader.cpp getEntryPoint m_entryTarget"}, {"t": "Front-end lowering passes", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-18", "x": "API-level cleanups that don't depend on the GPU: structured-CFG merges, inline everything, lower SPIR-V access chains and terminators, then (further down) globals, memory ops and math. Afterwards the stage modules are linked into one pipeline module. llpc/lowering/Lowering.cpp LowerCfgMerges LowerAccessChain AlwaysInlinerPass"}, {"t": "LLPC \u2192 LGC: pipeline->generate()", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-19", "x": "One LLVM module now represents the whole pipeline.  generate  runs the LGC middle end and LLVM codegen and writes the ELF into  elfStream . llpc/context/llpcCompiler.cpp pipeline->generate"}, {"t": "PipelineState::generate: lower, then codegen", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-20", "x": "The two halves of the back end.  LgcLowering::addPasses  runs the hardware-aware middle-end passes on a new-PM pass manager.  addTargetPasses  then sets up the LLVM AMDGPU code generator on a legacy pass manager that writes the object file. lgc/state/Compiler.cpp LgcLowering::addPasses addTargetPasses"}, {"t": "Pipeline-state-dependent LGC passes", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-21", "x": "These passes are why AMDVLK compiles whole pipelines.  LowerVertexFetch  needs the vertex input formats,  LowerFragmentColorExport  needs the color attachment formats and blend state, and  LowerDesc  needs the descriptor layout.  CheckShaderCache  ( cache #4 , per-stage) can skip codegen for stages already compiled.  AddBufferOperationMetadata  runs only on GFX12+. lgc/lowering/LgcLowering.cpp LowerVertexFetch LowerFragmentColorExport LowerDesc CheckShaderCache AddBufferOperationMetadata"}, {"t": "GFX difference: NGG", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-22", "x": "On GFX11 and later, NGG (primitive shaders) is  mandatory ; the legacy VS/GS hardware path is gone. On GFX10 it is on unless disabled by a flag. Before this,  PreparePipelineAbi  has already merged API stages into hardware stages (e.g. VS+GS \u2192 one NGG primitive shader) and laid out the PAL ABI. lgc/lowering/LgcLowering.cpp usesNgg gfxIp.major >= 11"}, {"t": "GFX IP \u2192 LLVM CPU name", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-23", "x": "{11, 0, 0} becomes  gfx1100 , {12, 0, 1} becomes  gfx1201 . LGC accepts only names in its  GpuNameMap  table, which starts at  gfx1010 . lgc/state/LgcContext.cpp gpuNameStream << \"gfx\""}, {"t": "Create the AMDGPU TargetMachine", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-24", "x": "The triple is  amdgcn--amdpal : the AMDGPU backend with the PAL OS ABI. That ABI determines how the ELF's metadata note is written, and it is what PAL's loader expects. lgc/state/LgcContext.cpp amdgcn--amdpal lookupTarget"}, {"t": "Hand-off to the LLVM AMDGPU backend", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-25", "x": "From here, standard LLVM code generation (instruction selection, register allocation, scheduling) produces ISA for the chosen gfx target. Those internals are  not traced  on this site. lgc/state/LgcContext.cpp addPassesToEmitFile"}, {"t": "AsmPrinter writes PAL metadata", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-26", "x": "For each hardware-stage entry function, the backend records the entry symbol, VGPR/SGPR counts and (on older PAL ABI versions) the  RSRC1 / RSRC2  register values. This is how PAL later learns how to program the shader registers without understanding the ISA. llvm/lib/Target/AMDGPU/AMDGPUAsmPrinter.cpp setEntryPoint setNumUsedVgprs setNumUsedSgprs"}, {"t": "Metadata \u2192 ELF note", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-27", "x": "Modern PAL metadata is serialized as MsgPack into an ELF note. PAL reads it back with  PipelineAbiReader  and  MsgPackReader . llvm/lib/Target/AMDGPU/Utils/AMDGPUPALMetadata.cpp toMsgPackBlob"}, {"t": "XGL creates the PAL pipeline from the ELF", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-28", "x": "The ELF goes into  Pal::GraphicsPipelineCreateInfo::pPipelineBinary , along with dynamic-state-related info XGL collected, and PAL constructs an  IPipeline . icd/api/vk_graphics_pipeline.cpp pPipelineBinary CreateGraphicsPipeline"}, {"t": "PAL: route to the HWL", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-29", "x": "The core device forwards to the GFX device, which unpacks archive libraries if any and creates the HWL-specific pipeline object. src/core/device.cpp CreateGraphicsPipelineUnpackArchiveLibs"}, {"t": "GraphicsPipeline::Init from the binary", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-30", "x": "PAL keeps a copy of the ELF and calls  InitFromPipelineBinary , which reads the metadata (already unpacked by  PipelineAbiReader ) and calls the HWL's  HwlInit . src/core/hw/gfxip/graphicsPipeline.cpp InitFromPipelineBinary"}, {"t": "HWL: relocate + upload code, build registers", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-31", "x": " CodeObjectUploader  copies the ELF's code and data sections into GPU memory ( pipelinePreferredHeap ) and applies relocations. The user-data layout and per-stage register values come from the metadata. The GFX10/11 HWL does the same in  Gfx9::GraphicsPipeline::HwlInit . src/core/hw/gfxip/gfx12/gfx12GraphicsPipeline.cpp CodeObjectUploader PerformRelocationsAndUploadToGpuMemory"}, {"t": "vkCreateCommandPool \u2192 ICmdAllocator", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-1", "x": "Each  VkCommandPool  owns a PAL  ICmdAllocator . It hands out fixed-size  chunks  of GPU memory for command data (PM4), embedded data (constants and descriptors written inline) and scratch.  allocSize  is the size of each GPU allocation and  suballocSize  the size of each chunk carved out of it.  autoMemoryReuse  lets chunks be recycled once the GPU is done with them. icd/api/vk_cmd_pool.cpp CommandDataAlloc EmbeddedDataAlloc suballocSize autoMemoryReuse"}, {"t": "vkBeginCommandBuffer", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-2", "x": "Unwraps the dispatchable  VkCommandBuffer  and calls  CmdBuffer::Begin , which resets XGL's tracked state and then begins the PAL command buffer. icd/api/entry.cpp ApiCmdBuffer::ObjectFromHandle"}, {"t": "Begin the PAL command buffer(s)", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-3", "x": "One PAL command buffer per GPU in the device mask.  ICmdBuffer::Begin  resets the PAL command streams. Their first chunk is requested lazily on the first command. icd/api/vk_cmdbuffer.cpp PalCmdBuffer(deviceIdx)->Begin"}, {"t": "vkCmdDraw", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-4", "x": "Note the argument re-ordering: Vulkan's (vertexCount, instanceCount, firstVertex, firstInstance) becomes XGL's (firstVertex, vertexCount, firstInstance, instanceCount). icd/api/entry.cpp ->Draw("}, {"t": "CmdBuffer::Draw: flush API state, then draw", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-5", "x": " ValidateGraphicsStates  pushes any Vulkan dynamic state that changed since the last draw (viewports, scissors, depth bias, etc.) into PAL calls. Then  PalCmdDraw  issues the draw. icd/api/vk_cmdbuffer.cpp ValidateGraphicsStates PalCmdDraw"}, {"t": "PalCmdDraw \u2192 ICmdBuffer::CmdDraw per GPU", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-6", "x": "For device groups, the draw is recorded into the PAL command buffer of every GPU in  m_curDeviceMask . icd/api/vk_cmdbuffer.cpp PalCmdBuffer(deviceIdx)->CmdDraw"}, {"t": "ICmdBuffer::CmdDraw is a function-pointer call", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-7", "x": "PAL's hot-path commands aren't virtual functions. They go through  m_funcTable , which the HWL fills with template instantiations specialized for the GFX level, whether PM4 optimization is on, whether view instancing is used, and so on. A draw ends up in a function with almost no runtime branches. inc/core/palCmdBuffer.h m_funcTable.pfnCmdDraw"}, {"t": "HWL CmdDraw: validate state", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-8", "x": "(GFX10\u201311 HWL, which lives in the gfx9 directory.)  ValidateDraw  writes every piece of dirty pipeline, user-data and render state as  SET_SH_REG / SET_CONTEXT_REG  packets. The exact register sequence is a large template and is  not traced  line by line here. src/core/hw/gfxip/gfx9/gfx9UniversalCmdBuffer.cpp ValidateDraw<IsGfx11, false, Pm4OptEnabled>"}, {"t": "Reserve space, build the packet, commit", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-9", "x": " m_deCmdStream  is the draw-engine command stream.  ReserveCommands  returns a pointer into the current chunk with guaranteed headroom, packet builders write DWORDs through it, and  CommitCommands  advances the write pointer. Instance count and first vertex/instance were already written as registers during validation, so the packet only needs the vertex count. src/core/hw/gfxip/gfx9/gfx9UniversalCmdBuffer.cpp BuildDrawIndexAuto CommitCommands"}, {"t": "CmdUtil::BuildDrawIndexAuto: the packet", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-10", "x": "Three DWORDs. DW0 is a type-3 header with opcode  IT_DRAW_INDEX_AUTO  (0x2D) and count = 3 \u2212 2 = 1. DW1 is  index_count . DW2 is a  VGT_DRAW_INITIATOR  value with SOURCE_SELECT = AUTO_INDEX. The annotated breakdown is below the tracer. src/core/hw/gfxip/gfx9/gfx9CmdUtil.cpp IT_DRAW_INDEX_AUTO DI_SRC_SEL_AUTO_INDEX draw_initiator"}, {"t": "Type3Header", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-11", "x": "Every type-3 packet goes through this helper.  count  is defined as the packet's total DWORD count minus 2, i.e. payload DWORDs \u2212 1. src/core/hw/gfxip/gfx9/gfx9CmdUtil.cpp header.count          = (count - 2); header.type           = 3;"}, {"t": "CmdStream::ReserveCommands", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-12", "x": "Reserves  ReserveLimit()  DWORDs. If the current chunk lacks the space, the stream chains to a new chunk. Chunks of one stream are linked so the CP can follow them. src/core/cmdStream.cpp ReserveLimit"}, {"t": "CmdStream::GetNextChunk", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-13", "x": "A stream first reuses its own  retained  chunks (kept across resets). Otherwise it asks the  CmdAllocator  for a fresh chunk. src/core/cmdStream.cpp m_retainedChunkList GetNewChunk"}, {"t": "CmdAllocator: free list \u2192 reuse list \u2192 new allocation", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-14", "x": "Chunk recycling. The free list holds chunks known to be idle. The reuse list holds chunks returned by reset command buffers; each is checked with  IsIdleOnGpu() , because the GPU may still be executing it. Only if both lists are exhausted does PAL make a new GPU allocation. src/core/cmdAllocator.cpp freeList reuseList IsIdleOnGpu busyList"}, {"t": "CmdAllocator::CreateAllocation", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-15", "x": "One  CmdStreamAllocation  (a GPU memory object of  allocSize ) is cut into  numChunks  chunks. The first is returned and the rest go on the free list. src/core/cmdAllocator.cpp allocCreateInfo.numChunks freeList.PushBack"}, {"t": "Compute: DISPATCH_DIRECT", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-16", "x": " vkCmdDispatch  \u2192  CmdBuffer::Dispatch  \u2192  ICmdBuffer::CmdDispatch  \u2192 HWL  CmdDispatch  \u2192 this builder. The packet carries the three group counts and a  COMPUTE_DISPATCH_INITIATOR ;  CS_W32_EN  selects wave32. src/core/hw/gfxip/gfx9/gfx9CmdUtil.cpp IT_DISPATCH_DIRECT dim_x dispatch_initiator"}, {"t": "vkCmdPipelineBarrier \u2192 release/acquire", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-17", "x": "On GFX10+ ( useReleaseAcquire ), XGL expresses Vulkan barriers in PAL's acquire/release model.  ExecuteAcquireRelease  translates stage and access masks plus image layouts and calls  ICmdBuffer::CmdReleaseThenAcquire . icd/api/vk_cmdbuffer.cpp m_flags.useReleaseAcquire ExecuteAcquireRelease"}, {"t": "GfxCmdBuffer \u2192 BarrierMgr", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-18", "x": "Barriers ignore predication. The HWL's  BarrierMgr  works out what the transition needs: waits on end-of-pipe events, cache write-back/invalidate, and metadata decompression blits. src/core/hw/gfxip/gfxCmdBuffer.cpp m_barrierMgr.ReleaseThenAcquire"}, {"t": "BarrierMgr::IssueReleaseThenAcquireSync", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-19", "x": "The source stage mask becomes  release events  (e.g. a RELEASE_MEM end-of-pipe event). The destination stage mask becomes an  acquire point  (how early the CP must wait), and the cache flags become GL2/GLx operations. These end up as RELEASE_MEM/ACQUIRE_MEM packets. src/core/hw/gfxip/gfx9/gfx9AcquireReleaseBarrier.cpp GetAcquirePoint GetReleaseEvents ReserveCommands"}, {"t": "ACQUIRE_MEM packet", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-20", "x": " ACQUIRE_MEM  (0x58) makes the CP wait and/or perform cache actions before later work. Note the assert: on GFX11 the RB (color/depth) caches can no longer be flushed through ACQUIRE_MEM. That has to happen at release time. src/core/hw/gfxip/gfx9/gfx9CmdUtil.cpp IT_ACQUIRE_MEM coher_cntl isGfx11"}, {"t": "vkQueueSubmit", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-1", "x": "Both  vkQueueSubmit  and  vkQueueSubmit2  go to one template,  Queue::Submit<SubmitInfoType> . icd/api/vk_queue.cpp Submit<VkSubmitInfo>"}, {"t": "Wait semaphores first", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-2", "x": "In  PalWaitSemaphores , each wait becomes  IQueue::WaitQueueSemaphore  with a point value: 0 for binary semaphores, the requested value for timeline semaphores. On amdgpu this doesn't block. It queues the wait so the next CS ioctl carries it (see below). icd/api/vk_queue.cpp WaitQueueSemaphore pointValue"}, {"t": "Build the PAL SubmitInfo", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-3", "x": "XGL collects each  VkCommandBuffer 's  Pal::ICmdBuffer  into  ppCmdBuffers . Note  gpuMemRefCount = 0 : Vulkan never passes per-submit memory references, because everything was made resident at allocation time ( Device::AddMemReference ). icd/api/vk_queue.cpp Pal::PerSubQueueSubmitInfo gpuMemRefCount"}, {"t": "PalQueueSubmit \u2192 IQueue::Submit", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-4", "x": "Unless  multiSubmitChaining  is enabled, XGL submits command buffers one at a time and attaches the fence only to the last one. After the submit, signal semaphores go through  PalSignalSemaphores  \u2192  IQueue::SignalQueueSemaphore . icd/api/vk_queue.cpp pPalQueue->Submit(singleSubmitInfo) ppFences"}, {"t": "PAL core Queue: fences, then OsSubmit", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-5", "x": " IQueue::Submit  is  Queue::SubmitInternal  ( queue.h ). After validation and command-buffer bookkeeping, it associates each fence with the submission context and calls the OS layer. If the queue is stalled waiting on a semaphore, it enqueues the submit instead. src/core/queue.cpp AssociateWithContext OsSubmit"}, {"t": "Amdgpu::Queue::OsSubmit: residency + engine split", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-6", "x": "Unless  alwaysResident  is set, PAL builds the per-submit resource (BO) list from the device-global and per-queue references. Further down, universal/compute queues take  SubmitPm4  and DMA (SDMA) queues take  SubmitNonGfxIp . src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuQueue.cpp UpdateResourceList"}, {"t": "UpdateResourceList: global refs \u2192 BO list", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-7", "x": "If nothing changed since the last submit, the previous list is reused. Otherwise the list is rebuilt from  m_globalRefMap , which holds every  VkDeviceMemory  that XGL added. Memory created  VM_ALWAYS_VALID  is skipped: the kernel keeps it valid in the process VM regardless. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuQueue.cpp m_globalRefMap m_globalRefDirty"}, {"t": "Each command stream becomes an IB", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-8", "x": "PAL submits only the  first chunk  of each command stream as an IB, sized by  CmdDwordsToExecute() . The remaining chunks are reached by chaining packets that PAL wrote at the end of each chunk. SubmitPm4 also adds internal preamble/postamble streams around the app's command buffers. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuQueue.cpp GetFirstChunk GpuVirtAddr AddIb"}, {"t": "AddIb: fill drm_amdgpu_cs_chunk_ib", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-9", "x": "An IB is just {GPU VA, size in bytes, IP type, ring, flags}. The IP type comes from the PAL engine type, which is how a submission reaches a  ring : GFX, COMPUTE or DMA. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuQueue.cpp va_start ib_bytes ip_type GetIpType"}, {"t": "Engine \u2192 ring type", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-10", "x": "Universal (graphics) queues \u2192  AMDGPU_HW_IP_GFX , compute queues \u2192  AMDGPU_HW_IP_COMPUTE  (MEC/ACE rings), DMA queues \u2192  AMDGPU_HW_IP_DMA  (SDMA). The kernel's scheduler picks a hardware ring instance within the IP. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuQueue.cpp AMDGPU_HW_IP_GFX AMDGPU_HW_IP_COMPUTE AMDGPU_HW_IP_DMA"}, {"t": "SubmitIbsRaw: IB chunks first", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-11", "x": "The CS ioctl takes an array of typed  chunks . IB chunks come first. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuQueue.cpp AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_IB"}, {"t": "Timeline-syncobj wait chunk", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-12", "x": "The semaphore waits queued earlier ( m_waitSemList ) become  AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_SYNCOBJ_TIMELINE_WAIT  entries of {syncobj handle, point}. A binary semaphore is a syncobj waited at point 0. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuQueue.cpp AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_SYNCOBJ_TIMELINE_WAIT m_waitSemList"}, {"t": "Signal chunk: always includes the queue's own syncobj", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-13", "x": "Every submission signals the queue's  m_lastSignaledSyncObject  (an extra entry after any PAL-internal signal semaphores). Fences and Vulkan signal semaphores are later fed from this syncobj. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuQueue.cpp m_lastSignaledSyncObject AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_SYNCOBJ_TIMELINE_SIGNAL"}, {"t": "BO list: inline chunk (or legacy handle)", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-14", "x": "On current kernels the BO list goes inline as  AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_BO_HANDLES  (a  drm_amdgpu_bo_list_in  pointing to entries). On the legacy path, PAL first creates a list object with  DRM_AMDGPU_BO_LIST  and passes its handle. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuQueue.cpp AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_BO_HANDLES bo_info_ptr"}, {"t": "Device::SubmitRaw2", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-15", "x": "The returned sequence number ( pFence , stored as the context's last timestamp) identifies this submission. Legacy timestamp fences use it. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuDevice.cpp pfnAmdgpuCsSubmitRaw2"}, {"t": "libdrm: amdgpu_cs_submit_raw2 \u2192 DRM_AMDGPU_CS", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-16", "x": "Packs the chunk pointers into  union drm_amdgpu_cs  together with the context ID and issues  DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS . amdgpu/amdgpu_cs.c DRM_AMDGPU_CS cs.in.chunks cs.out.handle"}, {"t": "Kernel: amdgpu_cs_ioctl", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-17", "x": "The kernel side in one function.  pass1  copies and parses chunks;  pass2  handles IBs and syncobj dependencies;  parser_bos  validates and reserves every BO in the list (moving them into place if evicted);  vm_handling  makes sure page tables are current;  sync_rings  collects dependencies;  submit  queues the job. drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_cs.c amdgpu_cs_pass1 amdgpu_cs_pass2 amdgpu_cs_parser_bos amdgpu_cs_vm_handling amdgpu_cs_sync_rings"}, {"t": "pass1: chunk parsing", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-18", "x": "The chunk IDs PAL produced are matched here: IB chunks become job IBs and  AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_BO_HANDLES  builds the submission's BO list. Syncobj chunks are handled in pass2 (e.g.  amdgpu_cs_p2_syncobj_timeline_wait , next step). drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_cs.c AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_IB AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_BO_HANDLES"}, {"t": "pass2: timeline wait \u2192 job dependency", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-19", "x": "Each {handle, point} is looked up and its fence added as a dependency of the job. The GPU scheduler won't run the job until the fence signals, so waits cost no CPU time. drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_cs.c amdgpu_syncobj_lookup_and_add"}, {"t": "Queue the job on the GPU scheduler", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-20", "x": " cs->out.handle = seq  is the sequence number libdrm returns to PAL.  drm_sched_entity_push_job  hands the job to the DRM GPU scheduler. The ioctl returns now; execution is asynchronous. drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_cs.c drm_sched_entity_push_job cs->out.handle = seq"}, {"t": "Scheduler runs the job: amdgpu_job_run", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-21", "x": "Once dependencies are satisfied, the scheduler calls  amdgpu_job_run , which calls  amdgpu_ib_schedule  on the ring. drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_job.c amdgpu_ib_schedule"}, {"t": "amdgpu_ib_schedule: emit IBs + fence", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-22", "x": "For each IB, the ring-specific  emit_ib  writes a packet into the kernel ring buffer. Then  amdgpu_fence_emit  writes an end-of-pipe fence, which is what eventually signals the syncobjs. drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_ib.c amdgpu_ring_emit_ib amdgpu_fence_emit"}, {"t": "gfx_v11: INDIRECT_BUFFER packet in the ring", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-23", "x": "The handoff to hardware. A PM4  INDIRECT_BUFFER  packet with PAL's IB address and the  VMID  in the control word (so the CP translates the IB's VAs through this process's page tables) is written to the ring. The CP then fetches and executes PAL's PM4, including the  DRAW_INDEX_AUTO  from Topic 4. GFX12's equivalent is  gfx_v12_0_ring_emit_ib_gfx . drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/gfx_v11_0.c PACKET3_INDIRECT_BUFFER vmid << 24"}, {"t": "VkFence \u2192 Pal::IFence", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-24", "x": "The same size-then-placement pattern. One PAL fence per GPU. icd/api/vk_fence.cpp GetFenceSize CreateFence"}, {"t": "amdgpu: SyncobjFence or legacy TimestampFence", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-25", "x": "On kernels with syncobj support (the normal case), a PAL fence wraps a DRM syncobj. The legacy  TimestampFence  instead waits on a (context, ring, sequence-number) triple. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuDevice.cpp SyncobjFence TimestampFence"}, {"t": "Signal the fence from the submission's syncobj", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-26", "x": "After the CS ioctl, the fence's syncobj gets a copy of the fence just attached to the queue's  m_lastSignaledSyncObject  ( ConveySyncObjectState  \u2192  amdgpu_cs_syncobj_transfer ).  vkWaitForFences  then waits on the syncobj ( WaitForSyncobjFences ). src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuQueue.cpp ConveySyncObjectState GetLastSignaledSyncObj"}, {"t": "VkSemaphore: binary or timeline", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-27", "x": "A  VkSemaphoreTypeCreateInfo  with  VK_SEMAPHORE_TYPE_TIMELINE  sets  Pal::QueueSemaphoreCreateInfo::flags.timeline  and the initial value. Otherwise it's binary. Both become a  Pal::IQueueSemaphore  via  CreateQueueSemaphore . icd/api/vk_semaphore.cpp palCreateInfo.flags.timeline VK_SEMAPHORE_TYPE_TIMELINE"}, {"t": "IQueueSemaphore = DRM syncobj", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-28", "x": "With  SemaphoreType::SyncObj , each semaphore, binary or timeline, is a DRM syncobj created by  CreateSyncObject  (\u2192  amdgpu_cs_create_syncobj2 ).  ProOnly  is a legacy amdgpu-pro semaphore type. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuDevice.cpp SemaphoreType::SyncObj CreateSyncObject"}, {"t": "Queue::WaitSemaphore just records the wait", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-29", "x": "For syncobj semaphores the wait is appended to  m_waitSemList  and becomes an  AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_SYNCOBJ_TIMELINE_WAIT  chunk of the next CS ioctl. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuQueue.cpp m_waitSemList.PushBack"}, {"t": "Queue::SignalSemaphore transfers from the last submit", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-30", "x": "Signalling (binary point 0 or a timeline point) copies the fence of the queue's last submission into the semaphore's syncobj at that point. If the queue has a pending wait, or has never submitted, PAL first does a dummy submission so there is a fence to transfer. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuQueue.cpp ConveySyncObjectState DummySubmit"}, {"t": "BO \u2014 Buffer Object", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-bo", "x": "A kernel-managed allocation of GPU-accessible memory (a DRM GEM object backed by TTM). Created by  AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE ; user space refers to it by a handle."}, {"t": "IB \u2014 Indirect Buffer", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-ib", "x": "A chunk of PM4 commands in GPU memory. User space submits IBs by GPU VA and size; the kernel emits an  INDIRECT_BUFFER  packet on a ring that points at it."}, {"t": "GART \u2014 Graphics Address Remapping Table", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-gart", "x": "System (host) memory made GPU-accessible through GPU page tables. The kernel calls this domain GTT. PAL exposes it as two heaps:  GpuHeapGartUswc  (write-combined) and  GpuHeapGartCacheable  (CPU-cached)."}, {"t": "GTT \u2014 Graphics Translation Table", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-gtt", "x": "The amdgpu kernel's name for the system-memory domain ( AMDGPU_GEM_DOMAIN_GTT ). PAL's GART heaps map to it."}, {"t": "VRAM \u2014 Video RAM", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-vram", "x": "Device-local memory ( AMDGPU_GEM_DOMAIN_VRAM ). PAL splits it into  GpuHeapLocal  (CPU-visible BAR window) and  GpuHeapInvisible ."}, {"t": "USWC \u2014 Uncached Speculative Write-Combining", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-uswc", "x": "A CPU caching mode: writes are combined and streamed, and reads are uncached and slow. Good for CPU\u2192GPU upload buffers. Requested with  AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE_CPU_GTT_USWC ."}, {"t": "VA \u2014 GPU Virtual Address", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-va", "x": "An address in the process's GPU virtual address space. Reserving a VA (libdrm  amdgpu_va_range_alloc ) is separate from backing it with a BO and mapping it ( AMDGPU_GEM_VA )."}, {"t": "PM4 \u2014 Packet Manager 4 command format", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-pm4", "x": "The command format the GPU command processor parses. Type-3 packets have a 32-bit header (opcode, payload count, shader type, predicate) followed by payload DWORDs."}, {"t": "CP \u2014 Command Processor", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-cp", "x": "The GPU front-end microcontrollers (PFP/ME for graphics, MEC for compute) that fetch and execute PM4."}, {"t": "LLPC \u2014 LLVM-Based Pipeline Compiler", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-llpc", "x": "AMD's Vulkan pipeline compiler library. Its front end translates SPIR-V to LLVM IR; it also contains LGC and drives the LLVM AMDGPU backend."}, {"t": "LGC \u2014 LLPC middle end (\"LLVM-based Graphics Compiler\")", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-lgc", "x": "LLPC's middle end ( llpc/lgc ). It lowers  lgc::Builder  operations into AMDGPU-ready IR using the pipeline state (vertex formats, color formats, descriptor layout, GFX IP) and emits the PAL pipeline ABI."}, {"t": "XGL \u2014 Vulkan API layer", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-xgl", "x": "The repo that implements the Vulkan ICD for AMDVLK ( icd/api ). It translates Vulkan calls into PAL calls."}, {"t": "PAL \u2014 Platform Abstraction Library", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-pal", "x": "AMD's shared GPU abstraction layer, used by several AMD drivers. It hides hardware generation (HWL) and OS (amdgpu) details."}, {"t": "HWL \u2014 Hardware Layer", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-hwl", "x": "PAL's per-GPU-generation implementation ( hw/gfxip/gfx9 ,  hw/gfxip/gfx12 ) that builds PM4 and register state."}, {"t": "ICD \u2014 Installable Client Driver", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-icd", "x": "A Vulkan driver loaded by the Khronos loader through a JSON manifest. AMDVLK's ICD is XGL."}, {"t": "syncobj \u2014 DRM synchronization object", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-syncobj", "x": "A kernel container for a dma_fence, optionally a timeline of fences indexed by a 64-bit point. Used for Vulkan fences, binary semaphores and timeline semaphores."}, {"t": "SDMA \u2014 System DMA engine", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-sdma", "x": "A copy engine with its own ring ( AMDGPU_HW_IP_DMA ). It runs PAL's DMA queues (Vulkan transfer queues)."}, {"t": "NGG \u2014 Next-Generation Geometry", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-ngg", "x": "The primitive-shader geometry pipeline of GFX10+. LGC generates it and makes it mandatory on GFX11+."}, {"t": "ELF \u2014 Executable and Linkable Format", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-elf", "x": "The container LLPC emits: ISA in .text plus a PAL metadata note (MsgPack) describing register values, user-data layout and resource usage."}, {"t": "SRD \u2014 Shader Resource Descriptor", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-srd", "x": "A 4- or 8-DWORD hardware descriptor for a buffer, image or sampler, read by shaders from descriptor memory."}, {"t": "SPIR-V \u2014 Standard Portable IR (Vulkan shader binary)", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-spirv", "x": "The shader IR an application passes to  vkCreateShaderModule . LLPC parses it with its SPIRV reader."}, {"t": "ISA \u2014 Instruction Set Architecture", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-isa", "x": "The GPU machine code (e.g. RDNA3 for gfx11) produced by the LLVM AMDGPU backend."}, {"t": "GEM \u2014 Graphics Execution Manager", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-gem", "x": "The DRM core's buffer-object framework. amdgpu BOs are GEM objects."}, {"t": "TTM \u2014 Translation Table Manager", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-ttm", "x": "The DRM memory manager that places and evicts BOs between VRAM and system memory. Not traced in detail on this site."}, {"t": "MEC \u2014 Micro Engine Compute", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-mec", "x": "The CP block that runs compute queues (ACE). PAL compute queues map to  AMDGPU_HW_IP_COMPUTE ."}, {"t": "residency \u2014 memory residency", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-residency", "x": "Guaranteeing that every BO a submission might touch is present and mapped while the GPU runs. On amdgpu this is done with the per-submit BO list, or by creating BOs  VM_ALWAYS_VALID ."}];
